bugs
- tests are checked with strict on except noImplicitAny (tsconfig.tests.json, tsconfig.node.json):
  about 1,000 untyped helper parameters and `let`s remain. Turn it on file by file (it was 721
  errors in 50 files when tried); typed builders (tests/builders.ts) and must() are in place
- noUncheckedIndexedAccess was tried on the app project: 142 errors, mostly false positives
  (regex groups after a match, indexes checked against length just above), so it is off. If it
  is revisited: setlist-page.ts (45) is where `this.songs[i]` is the likeliest real bug
- Nashville-number charts read from the key's root, so a minor key is wrong: with `{key: Bm}`,
  6m 5 4 becomes G#m F# E (`convertDegreeToNote`, transpose.ts, uses only the root; Bm would need the
  relative major, D, giving Bm A G). Decide whether SongSelect numbers a minor song from the minor root
  or from the relative major, then fix and test both. Letters stay the stored format (custom
  non-SongSelect sheets are letters, and transposing letters is well tested), so numbers are only read.
  Background: charts imported by the bookmarklet before its fix were numbers under `{key: C}`, which
  made "See His Love" show Em D C for Gm (Sunday 18 Oct). Reimport the nine such charts in the Gateway
  Church space: Adonai, All Hail King Jesus, Here Is Love, Holy Holy Holy, More Precious Than Silver,
  Our God He Reigns, See His Love, Washed, What A Friend.
browser support
- Decided (2026-10-10): the floor is iOS/Safari 17 and Chrome/Edge 120, set in .browserslistrc
  and TARGET in vite.config.ts (keep them the same). Reasoning: support devices up to about five
  years old; asking people to update their OS is fine, excluding a device that cannot update is
  not, and every iPhone/iPad from 2021 on can run iOS 17 and later (a survey of the worship team:
  all on 26+, lowest 26.5), but some people dislike updating, so the floor is what the code
  needs with margin, not the newest. An audit (grep for version-gated features) found nothing
  between 26.2 and 16.2 that costs anything: the first thing below that is color-mix() (42 uses).
  View transitions are feature-detected. The 2017 iPad (16.7), the Air 2 and the 2019-2020 models
  stuck on iOS 18 or lower are mostly outside the window on purpose. The floor is a claim
  from reading the code, not tested on an old device: do that (Playwright's WebKit is current)
- A user reported iPadOS 15.8.8 (2026-10-11): so an iPad Air 2 (2014) or iPad mini 4 (2015), the
  last models on iPadOS 15, which cannot update. Outside the window on purpose, so nothing is
  done for now. If it is revisited: lower TARGET and .browserslistrc to Safari 15.4, give the 42
  color-mix() uses fallback colours (Safari 16.2), test on a real device (a module using class
  static blocks or regex lookbehind, Safari 16.4, would not even parse: unknown). The "update
  your device" page below is the cheap part either way
- No fallbacks or polyfills for what 17 has (nesting, dialog, :has, view transitions as an
  enhancement). Look through the code for workarounds the floor makes unnecessary
- Below the floor the app has no message: a browser older than 17 gets whatever happens when
  it meets syntax or APIs it lacks (first real case: an iPhone on 18.0.1 reported by the team,
  which is above this floor but was asked to update to 27: check it works). Show a plain "Update
  your device" page instead: a small classic script in index.html (it runs anywhere) that
  feature-detects what the floor guarantees (e.g. CSS.supports for nesting or :has(), and
  structuredClone) and replaces the page with the required versions. Find out first what an
  older device actually shows
- Core is setlist navigation (reading and moving through the setlist, offline). The media player
  is optional and may be off on a device without Web Audio: it is not loaded when the device has
  none or it is switched off in Preferences (media-player-loader.ts), and a player that fails to
  load is a warning, not an error. Don't make the core depend on it
- Usage pattern to design for: a dedicated iPad opened every 3 to 4 weeks, often on poor wifi.
  So: the app must open offline from its cache (done: the build precaches everything,
  tests/built.playwright.ts), data must survive the weeks between (the install nag, below), and
  a signed-in session and the Drive link must still work after weeks (test an expired token with
  no network: local setlists must still show)

navigation
- When the supported browsers all have the Navigation API (Safari/iOS 26.2, Chrome 102; the floor
  is Safari/iOS 17 now, and Firefox, from 147, is not targeted), rewrite AppRouter on it and delete the code the platform then does: link interception,
  popstate, history entry keys and the view state in sessionStorage (use the entry's state), and
  the scroll/focus handling. Keep AppRouter the only place that knows. No polyfill for now:
  @virtualstate/navigation is still alpha (last release Oct 2025), doesn't intercept link clicks
  (so we'd keep that code), and passes 139 of 306 web-platform tests
- Swiping (library <-> song, setlist songs) is the browser's own touch scroll with CSS scroll-snap;
  we only watch where it ended with an IntersectionObserver (library-view.ts, setlist-page.ts), which
  can't tell a swipe from our own scrollIntoView, so library-view.ts suppresses it for 600ms around
  programmatic scrolls (#suppressScrollResetTemporarily). That timer raced going forward in history
  under load (fixed, see docs/test-catches.jsonl). If it causes more trouble: use `scrollend`
  (Chrome/Edge 114+, Safari 17.4+; the floor is Safari 17, so Safari 17.0-17.3 needs a fallback) in
  place of the observer and the timer. Custom touch/pointer code is only for drags that must stop
  native scrolling: overview reordering (touch-action: none) and the media player

