UI
- recover when Safari has cleared the storage: the nag is the prevention, this is the cure.
  The JWE key stays in the worker (JWE_ENCRYPTION_KEY): the worker needs it to decrypt the blob
  and use the Google refresh token, and the browser must never hold it. What the browser holds
  is the encrypted blob in IndexedDB (auth-db.ts), and that is what an eviction deletes, along
  with the spaces and their Drive-folder links (space-db.ts), the songs and
  setlists (db.ts) and the current-space marker (localStorage). So there is nothing on the device
  to restore from: recovery needs either the user or the server.
  - Plan (decided): the server keeps a copy of the blob in a cookie, and the app restores from it.
    - the worker also sets the blob as an HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite=Lax cookie (Path=/session,
      long Max-Age) on /oauth/callback, and clears it on /session/revoke. WebKit's tracking
      prevention (webkit.org/tracking-prevention) caps cookies created in JavaScript, not
      Set-Cookie ones (except under CNAME cloaking to a third party), so it survives the eviction
    - POST /session/restore reads the cookie and returns what /oauth/callback does (blob,
      access_token, user), so the client can store the blob again
    - detect an eviction: a localStorage marker ("this device has data") that is missing when the
      app opens, and /session/restore answers yes. Then show "Safari cleared this device's data:
      restoring your setlists from Google Drive" and run the existing first-sign-in sync
      (checkFirstTimeAuth renames the Personal space to the Google name, Sync joins the existing
      same-named Drive folder). No tap needed. If /session/restore says no (no cookie), fall back
      to asking the user to sign in again: google-auth.ts uses access_type: 'offline' with
      prompt: 'consent', so that should be issued a new refresh token (verify with a real wipe)
    - update public/privacy.html in the same change: it says Chordless uses no cookies and the
      worker stores nothing. Say it sets one sign-in cookie (the encrypted token, HttpOnly, how
      long it lasts, removed on sign-out and Clear Local Data)
    - needs the network, so say so plainly at church
    - it is an auth change: review it with the user before building
    - security: a same-origin check (Origin / Sec-Fetch-Site) on /session/*; no
      Access-Control-Allow-Credentials with a reflected origin; restrict the current
      `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (already on the production list); "Clear Local Data" and
      sign-out must clear the cookie too, or they would restore what the user wiped; check that
      chordless.app is not a CNAME to another registrable domain
    - tests: worker unit tests for set, restore and clear; a Playwright test that wipes
      IndexedDB and localStorage with the cookie kept and sees the restore
  - either way, unsynced changes and device settings (theme, pad set) are lost: sync early and
    often (see the background sync item)
- autoscaling font
- install nag ("install to keep your data on this device"). The welcome card (a new user's home
  page) already has an "Install it" step with the reason and the per-platform instructions
  (src/js/install.ts has the detection and the Chromium prompt): this is the nag that keeps asking
  after that, below. this will be important for a dedicated church
  iPad that is only opened when its owner is on the rota (every 3 to 4 weeks). Safari (iOS, and
  macOS) deletes a site's script-writable storage (IndexedDB, localStorage, the service worker
  and its caches) after 7 days of Safari use without the user interacting with the site (days
  the browser is used, not calendar days: a rarely used iPad is not wiped just by sitting idle,
  but Safari use on other days counts; WebKit's "Full Third-Party Cookie Blocking and More"
  post, 2020; not re-checked since) unless the site is added to the Home Screen, which has its
  own counter. What is lost: the sign-in, the space's Drive folder link, unsynced changes, settings
  and pads, and at church, with poor wifi, the setlists are gone until signed in and synced.
  On iOS and iPadOS in a Safari tab (not navigator.standalone) it should INSIST: not a corner
  banner that "Not now" dismisses for weeks, but a sheet over the home page that explains the
  risk in one line and walks through Share › Add to Home Screen, back each time the app is
  opened in Safari until it is installed. Allow "Continue in Safari anyway" for that visit only
  (people borrow an iPad, or are mid-rota). Never over a setlist (the home page only). Detect
  iOS with the user agent or, for iPadOS (which reports as a Mac), maxTouchPoints > 1.
  The platform detection, the Chromium prompt and the instructions already exist in
  src/js/install.ts and the welcome card: reuse them. Skip when already installed. Installing
  also lets Chrome grant persistent storage.

- email replies: a logo where Gmail shows a profile picture needs BIMI, and BIMI needs a VMC or CMC
  certificate (from a CA such as DigiCert or Entrust, a yearly cost; Let's Encrypt cannot issue it).
  Without one Gmail shows the letter circle. The other parts: a DNS TXT at default._bimi.chordless.app
  pointing at an SVG Tiny PS logo served over HTTPS (public/ would do), DMARC at p=quarantine or
  reject, and SPF/DKIM that align with the domain (check that Cloudflare signs the Worker's replies
  with the domain's own DKIM key: not known). Not worth it for one church team; revisit if a
  certificate is bought for another reason.

library
 - ccli report nag
 - lyrics full-text search. Built (src/js/utils/song-search.ts, used by `filterSongs`): MiniSearch over
   the songs the library page and the add-song modal already have, with their charts parsed (title,
   artist, variant name, lyrics; each word is indexed as written and stemmed with `stemmer`, in lower
   case without accents or apostrophes, and the very common words are left out). It finds other forms
   of a word ("praises" finds "praise"), a slip of the fingers (fuzzy, on stems of six letters or more:
   on shorter ones "praises" would find "raise"), words in any order (all must be there), and a word
   begun (the last one: as written, or going on from a stem of four letters or more with the start of
   an ending, `ENDINGS`, and a lost "e": "worshippin" finds "worship", "lovi" finds "love"). The words
   found are marked in the title, artist and variant name, and the best line is quoted (`wordRanges`,
   `lyricSnippet`). Songs found by the words alone come after every song the typed letters are in,
   except that a title that has the word is ranked as a title that has the letters (`wordRank`); a
   song found only because a word in it has a letter wrong comes last of all; among lyrics-only
   matches the better score goes first. Checked over many words drawn from the songs, made-up and
   real (docs/TESTING.md, "The search, over many words"). Made in memory, not stored: it is rebuilt when the
   list of songs changes (the library page and the modal make it a moment after the list is shown,
   `warmSearch`), which costs about 0.2 s for 600 songs on a laptop. The list includes songs of the
   device's other databases, so one index covers "From …" songs too, and there is nothing to keep
   up to date after a save, a sync, or leaving a space. Not built:
   - matchLibrary's "line of the song's words" step (src/js/import-plan.ts) still scans; it could use
     the same index (rejoining SongSelect's split syllables is `SPLIT_WORD` in song-filter.ts)
   - if libraries grow so that making the index is felt (thousands of songs, an old iPad), store the
     serialised index (MiniSearch toJSON/loadJSON) in its own IndexedDB store as a derived cache that
     is safe to drop, one per database, updated per chart on save (db.saveChordPro/deleteChordPro)
     and by contentHash after a sync pull, with a version to bump when the tokeniser or stemmer changes
   - rank a title that is the word ("Praise" for "praises") above a title with the word in it
   - a word with an apostrophe for a letter left out ("ev'ry", "pow'r", "heav'n", "ne'er") is found as
     the word it is: indexed with each of a, e, i, o, u, v put back (`lettersPutBack`), and marked the
     same way. Not done: an everyday contraction (Lord's, don't) is left alone, as is an apostrophe at an
     end of a word ("singin'", "'cause", "'neath"), and a letter left out that is not one of those
     six or more than one letter ("fount'n" for "fountain"). Add what a real chart needs
   - a word begun that is only a stem and a short ending ("moti" is "mot" and "i", so "mote") finds
     the odd unrelated word; fine so far, look again if it is noticed

- Make the settings controls and panels (About: version, install, offline, storage status) more compact; they are all a little large.
- The info icon is an i-in-a-circle inside a second circle. Want one circle only: either just an `i` inside our current border circle, or the icon without the border.
- Shared with me (docs/SHARING.md, "Shared with me"): built the database, the home page group, and tidying (expiry, and dropping a setlist once the user has it in a space). Search across all databases (library page and add-song modal, tagged "From …") and "Add to my library" (a copy into the current space) are built. Still to do: deleting received songs by hand; warnings on space delete/Reset from Drive. Links are shorter (128-bit key + 6-character setlist code that sends space members straight to their own setlist).
- Data migrations with a UI (asked for 2026-10-20). Today's two (the spaces database `organisations` → `spaces`, in `SpaceDB.init`; and the file fields `driveFileId` → `fileRef` etc., in `migrateRecords` in `db.ts`) run by themselves when a database is opened, silently. More will come, so give them a shape: a registry (`src/js/migrations.ts`) of named, ordered, idempotent steps, each with `needed()`, `run(progress)` and a description; a record of what ran and when (and any failure) kept on the device; run the cheap ones at load behind a "Updating your data…" notice that does not stop a live setlist (CLAUDE.md: a toast, never a block); and a Storage page section, "Data updates", listing each step as done / waiting / failed with "Apply" (and "Try again") for the ones not run automatically. IndexedDB version upgrades (`onupgradeneeded`) cannot wait for a button: they stay automatic, but should be recorded in the same list. Decide first which migrations may be left for the user to apply (a big one, such as copying pad sets) and which must not.
